  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Consider the matrices $A$ and $B$ given below. Find $det(A)-det({B}^{T}).$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1.5 & 7.4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0.4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}|=(2)\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1=-4 \\\\ det(B) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1.5 & 7.4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0.4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|=3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $det({B}^{T})=det(B)=-6.$ \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(A)-det({B}^{T})=-4-(-6)=2.\n$$",
